#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012cd8a6-b675-5154-9601-7a65d5de12d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263012} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbf0d98-bbd6-5024-9ccd-0001e83cbc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263142} {:answer/id #uuid "16bba587-8f85-5f00-9ec1-694ef5daaab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263215} {:answer/id #uuid "35ebeeaa-8733-53f7-ba43-97e360520750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263476} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cbe7a9-8425-584f-8269-2ba210b98917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbf0d98-bbd6-5024-9ccd-0001e83cbc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263142} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4e0414-3c6f-5cb2-bb1f-c326aa8240ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4f57242-15d3-5f37-ad23-7508d1a49de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130369" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=3$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $-4\\text{det}({A}^{T})+2\\text{det}(I)=3$\n- $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=6$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=1$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-4\\text{det}({A}^{T})+2\\text{det}(I)=-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1=-10\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=3-1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb8b630-1858-5675-a8ea-afd745123540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272641} {:answer/id #uuid "25136c9e-920f-566e-a408-ea4a7af1ef37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272712} {:answer/id #uuid "28f41079-6423-5124-b7dd-2b748bc5ac50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272753} {:answer/id #uuid "37c41e9d-c90b-5532-80b8-70fb57bf191a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272875} {:answer/id #uuid "39e96dc4-16ab-55e3-b742-85d0597e033b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39e96dc4-16ab-55e3-b742-85d0597e033b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3f55ee-40e6-566b-9375-89a8f6437390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94fb05dd-7b6c-5fef-becd-0abc3ccf0dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158371" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176604ed-c837-5d39-bf7d-e8e2128262e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412055} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1ed2f9-1e46-534a-a381-abb6e053ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412056} {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd590b8-7f9d-5cdd-9e3e-23772a325ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412058} {:answer/id #uuid "e04a3a05-fb55-5020-b5ef-f2fc7abaa120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42481" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$\n- $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91b8ff9-f8eb-51b8-b52f-289c02818338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370955} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4a327-ea8e-530f-89f9-a4e243adc7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370956} {:answer/id #uuid "e926e6d7-8c6a-52a4-b903-1e9338a6f201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370957} {:answer/id #uuid "c331c706-c3f7-5678-9e57-a7714e5447c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370958} {:answer/id #uuid "7ede5ce4-29de-59fd-baf2-447d94e15852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91b8ff9-f8eb-51b8-b52f-289c02818338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370955} :answer-field/id #uuid "59058239-7698-5829-8ac2-6fc0b1fe2dc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a0d7e9-a72d-5fc5-b7f4-cd87aaa4ba3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42923" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =-1\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3))=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(F)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ea4053-49e8-5168-b098-82430469625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336121} {:answer/id #uuid "1f526be7-ec90-5c66-9047-fe096403b260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336321} {:answer/id #uuid "608cd9c2-29eb-5826-a7de-08dd4569f81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336906} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec811cf-1595-5300-be5a-c090541a7609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337179} {:answer/id #uuid "f66c976f-3fd2-55bb-96d7-c102515381cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec811cf-1595-5300-be5a-c090541a7609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337179} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27254a7-7a2d-59ff-8e48-3b8de8a30567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2c7d5db-866e-5c6e-9e0a-4bf934983da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-69980" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$H=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\,\\,\\,-3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(H) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\,\\,\\,-3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\,\\,\\,-3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\,\\,\\,-3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $1$st row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(H) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-6-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
